ok why the git commands in local computer terminal is required ?

This is probably the most important Git question you'll ask. Once you understand this, the entire purpose of Git becomes clear.

The short answer is:

GitHub stores your project online. Git (the commands in your terminal) manages your work on your computer and tells GitHub what to update.

Think of it like writing a document.

GitHub = Google Drive (stores your files online)
VS Code = Microsoft Word (where you write)
Git = The synchronization tool that tracks every change and uploads it safely
Why can't I just use GitHub?

You can for small edits.

For example, if you're only writing notes:

README.md
MCQs.md
Assignments.md

Editing directly on GitHub is perfectly fine.

But imagine you're building a real Data Engineering project.

Customer_ETL_Project
│
├── extract.py
├── transform.py
├── load.py
├── config.py
├── requirements.txt
└── README.md

Editing all these files one by one in a browser would be slow and difficult.

What does Git do on your computer?

Suppose you change three files.

transform.py
config.py
README.md

Git immediately knows:

Which files changed
Which lines changed
Who changed them
When they changed

That's why we use:

git status

It tells you exactly what's different.

Why do we need commands?

Imagine you're writing an assignment.

Without Git
Assignment.docx
Assignment_Final.docx
Assignment_Final2.docx
Assignment_Final3.docx
Assignment_ReallyFinal.docx

😄 This gets messy very quickly.

With Git
Version 1
      ↓
Version 2
      ↓
Version 3
      ↓
Version 4

Every version is stored as a commit.